---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  Block,
  Card,
  ComparisonTable,
  Grid,
  Links,
  PageHero,
  Section,
  SplitHeader,
  Stack,
  StackArrow,
  StackCells,
  StackNode,
  StackNote,
  Tabs,
  TintCard,
} from '../../components/ds';
import { LINKS } from '../../data/site';

const questions = [
  {
    label: 'What did it do?',
    them: 'Whatever the code said on the day it ran. You trace which columns fed which output by hand.',
    us: 'Column-level lineage resolved from the definition, back to leaf source columns. Each edge carries a named transform, so a derivation reads as `concat → geocode`.',
  },
  {
    label: 'When did it do it?',
    them: 'The job’s start time: when the machine woke up, not which version of the data it read.',
    us: 'Two stamps per refresh: a content hash of the definition, and the input version at the best fidelity the source offers (Iceberg snapshot, watermark, freshness token, or clock), with the fidelity recorded.',
  },
  {
    label: 'How do you audit it?',
    them: 'Code review, repeated every time anyone changes it.',
    us: 'A query. Stamps land in the refresh ledger and trace tables, joinable to the business data they describe.',
  },
  {
    label: 'What does it mean?',
    them: 'The name someone typed on the output table, and a wiki page written later by someone else.',
    us: 'A dataset is declared as an entity or a fact, with a key, a grain, and history.',
  },
];

const drift = [
  {
    label: 'A column is added',
    title: 'Nothing happens.',
    body: 'A definition names the columns it uses, and a new column is not referenced. On the landing side, incoming rows map to target columns by name and extra keys are dropped.',
  },
  {
    label: 'A column is removed',
    title: 'The refresh fails loudly, at that node.',
    body: 'If a definition referenced the column, the refresh stops there instead of producing a NULL column three hops downstream. If the share of expected columns collapses, the land is refused. A schema check on the contract catches it independently of any refresh.',
  },
  {
    label: 'A column changes type',
    title: 'The schema check fires.',
    body: 'The declared type no longer matches the observed one, which is exactly what that check is for.',
  },
  {
    label: 'A column changes meaning',
    title: 'The distribution checks fire.',
    body: 'No system reads intent. But a change consequential enough to move your numbers moves a distribution, and invalid, missing, duplicate, and aggregate checks fire when it does. Then ask what derives from that column and get the answer across every view and MV that consumes it.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Materialized views — analytics you declare — Provisa"
  description="Layered materialized views with recorded transforms, freshness-driven refresh, and transparent query rewrite — a declarative dependency graph instead of an orchestration DAG."
>
  <PageHero
    crumbs={[{ label: 'Provisa', href: '/' }, { label: 'Materialized views' }]}
    eyebrow="Materialized views"
    title="Declare the dataset. There is no pipeline to run."
    lede="A materialized view can sit on a source, a view, or another MV, stacking from raw source to final analytics table. Lineage, refresh, and governance come from the definition, so there is no orchestration DAG to write or babysit."
    actions={[
      { label: 'Book a demo', href: '#contact' },
      { label: 'Start a free trial', href: LINKS.cloud, variant: 'outline' },
    ]}
  >
    <Stack slot="aside" label="A chain of declared datasets">
      <StackCells items={['Postgres', 'MongoDB', 'S3 · Parquet']} />
      <StackArrow />
      <StackNode title="orders_enriched" meta="view · join + geocode" tone="cobalt" />
      <StackArrow />
      <StackNode title="daily_revenue" meta="MV · refresh on freshness" tone="forest" />
      <StackArrow />
      <StackNode title="monthly_close" meta="MV · refresh on calendar" tone="forest" />
      <StackNote label="GOVERNED">RLS and masking hold at every node</StackNote>
    </Stack>
  </PageHero>

  <Section tone="surface" labelledBy="origins-title">
    <Block>
      <SplitHeader eyebrow="Where pipelines come from" eyebrowTone="cobalt" title="Two reasons people write imperative code" titleId="origins-title">
        <p class="t-body-lg pv-body">
          Almost nobody writes a pipeline because they wanted a program. Both of the usual reasons can be handled by declaration instead.
        </p>
      </SplitHeader>
      <Grid min={360} gap={16}>
        <TintCard
          tone="cobalt"
          index="01"
          title="Shaping"
          plainTitle
          body="The data arrived in a shape SQL couldn’t address: nested JSON, documents with arrays, a directory of files, a stream. Non-relational data falls into a modest set of structure types, and each has a short declarative rule for projecting it into rows and columns. Once everything is two-dimensional, the rest is SQL, which a machine can read back."
        />
        <TintCard
          tone="forest"
          index="02"
          title="Moving data from A to B"
          plainTitle
          body="Writing that code forces a developer to decide which copy is authoritative, how stale is acceptable, and what happens when a pull fails. Here those are rules, settled once. A failed pull with no fresh cache is an error, not yesterday’s numbers. A write goes to the system of record and invalidates the cache. Re-pointing a source from Oracle to Postgres is a registration change."
        />
      </Grid>
    </Block>
  </Section>

  <Section labelledBy="audit-title">
    <Block gap={40}>
      <SplitHeader eyebrow="Auditability" eyebrowTone="vermilion" title="Four questions, answered from the definition" titleId="audit-title">
        <p class="t-body-lg pv-body">A pipeline tells you how a number was computed. A definition tells you what the number is.</p>
        <p class="t-body-lg pv-body">
          The declarative form also lets the system refuse definitions that could never be reproduced. A distributed MV that calls
          <code>now()</code> or <code>random()</code>, or takes a <code>LIMIT</code> with no total ordering, is rejected.
        </p>
      </SplitHeader>
      <ComparisonTable caption="Auditing an imperative pipeline and a declared dataset" rowHeader="Question" them="Imperative pipeline" us="Declared dataset" rows={questions} />
    </Block>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="surface" labelledBy="refresh-title">
    <Block>
      <SplitHeader eyebrow="Refresh and rewrite" eyebrowTone="forest" title="Two knobs, and queries that find the MV on their own" titleId="refresh-title">
        <p class="t-body-lg pv-body">
          Each MV records the SQL or join that built it and the input signals it read. Because the transform is recorded, queries against
          the underlying tables are rewritten onto a fresh MV when one covers them, partial join matches included. Nobody has to know the MV
          exists to benefit from it.
        </p>
      </SplitHeader>
      <Grid min={300} gap={16}>
        <Card
          title="Refresh by freshness"
          titleClass="t-h3"
          body="An MV rebuilds only when an upstream input changed, and the change fans out to its dependents. Demand-driven refresh is where a derived layer’s compute bill usually shrinks."
        />
        <Card
          title="Refresh by calendar"
          titleClass="t-h3"
          body="A TTL or interval, for when time is the trigger: a daily close, a monthly snapshot. There is no hand-maintained schedule to drift from the model."
        />
      </Grid>
    </Block>
  </Section>

  <Section labelledBy="drift-title">
    <Block gap={40}>
      <SplitHeader eyebrow="Schema drift" eyebrowTone="cobalt" title="What happens when the source changes" titleId="drift-title">
        <p class="t-body-lg pv-body">Drift is the standing objection to any declarative model. It deserves four separate answers.</p>
      </SplitHeader>
      <Tabs id="drift" label="Kinds of schema change" kicker="WHAT PROVISA DOES" tabs={drift} />
      <p class="t-body-lg pv-body pv-measure">
        Most drift arrives as a release a team shipped, so the failing refresh and the checks fire in a lower environment first. When the
        change comes from outside, like a vendor feed or a partner export, no system can prevent it. What you get is a refresh that fails
        instead of landing bad data, checks on the first bad batch, and a list of exactly what is downstream.
      </p>
    </Block>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="vermilion" labelledBy="hole-title">
    <SplitHeader eyebrow="Governance" title="A pipeline is a hole in your governance" titleId="hole-title">
      <p class="t-body-lg">
        A pipeline reads governed data and writes a new table somewhere else. That table has the same numbers, no row filter, no masking,
        and no record of who may see it. Every rule written upstream stops at the write, and nobody involved intended the bypass.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body-lg">
        A declared dataset never leaves the model. Row-level security, masking, and relationship rules hold on an MV exactly as they do on
        the source table.
      </p>
      <div style="padding-top: 8px">
        <Links items={[{ label: 'How governance reaches derived tables', href: '/why/governance' }]} />
      </div>
    </SplitHeader>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="surface" labelledBy="custom-title">
    <SplitHeader eyebrow="Custom logic" eyebrowTone="forest" title="Custom code is allowed. Undeclared custom code isn’t." titleId="custom-title">
      <p class="t-body-lg pv-body">
        Some work needs code the model cannot express: text from a PDF, an embedding from a model endpoint, a third-party API with its own
        rate limits. That code enters as a two-dimensional transform with named datasets in and a dataset out, and data quality checked on
        both sides.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body-lg pv-body">
        The call becomes a node in the graph with real edges, so lineage passes through it. A stored procedure declares only a signature and
        reads and writes whatever it likes. A contract names the data flowing in and out, and that is what lineage is made of.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body-lg pv-body">
        Where copies are genuinely needed (a backend for a frontend, a cache near a workload, another organization), read them from the
        published edge over any of the nine protocols. A copy made that way can be traced back to the model.
      </p>
      <div style="padding-top: 8px">
        <Links items={[{ label: 'Publishing lineage outward', href: '/why/catalog' }]} />
      </div>
    </SplitHeader>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
